WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.163
- Hi everyone.

00:00:01.163 --> 00:00:04.590
Sal Khan here and welcome
to Middle School Physics.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.790
I have Iman Howard, who manages
all of our STEM content.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.380
Iman, why should folks be excited

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.450
about Middle School Physics?

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.700
- So, Middle School Physics is like

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.800
the only science out there

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.410
that explains how things happen.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.140
And so basically,
everything's made of matter.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.940
Me, you,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.470
the chair that I'm sitting on.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.900
And this course is gonna
explore how we exist

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.100
in the natural world.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.330
So, for example,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.760
we talk a little bit
about movement in forces.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.980
And we learn that everything,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.260
everything that we have a collision with

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.230
has this equal but opposite
force that's applied

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.610
when the collision happens.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.340
So, that's why when you
give those high fives

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.990
and then your hand starts stinging,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.060
it's because the same
force you gave your buddy

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.070
is the same force they gave you back.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.200
And then we also talk about force

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.066
in a way where it doesn't touch you.

00:00:54.066 --> 00:00:56.340
So, like I'm thinking like Star Wars,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.488
there's like this force
energy, like gravitational.

00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:02.940
There's magnetic energy,
there's electric energy.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.110
And then finally,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.430
we get into waves.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:06.600
And we talk a little bit about

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.730
how waves, you know, exists.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.990
Whether it's sound waves

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.520
or even the waves in the ocean.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.830
What do you think's exciting about it?

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.910
- Oh, well,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.710
that's a dangerous question to ask me.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.150
I wanted to be a physicist

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.710
and I still aspire to be it.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.640
Because, you know,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.040
we kind of wake up in this cosmos.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.720
And we're just trying to understand what,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:25.890
where we fit in.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.850
And physics asks the most
fundamental questions

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.890
about how the universe works.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.470
And so when I first learned
about Newton's laws,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.330
and fields, and

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.220
all of the things that
you just touched on,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.900
it started to give me goose bumps.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.920
Because I'm like, wow,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.440
we can finally understand how
the universe fits together

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.150
and then use that to make predictions.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.760
And then think about things
that we don't understand.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.500
And there is so much
that we don't understand.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.330
So I think this is the beginning of a very

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.643
very exciting journey in physics.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.580
- I agree, Sal.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.744
(energetic blipping)

